From 59c065ea832d8d883007899baa457d0d5a6bb636 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Ahrens <matt@mahrens.org>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:13:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] ZIL replay can apply part of a split WR_NEED_COPY write

After a crash while an fsync() is still writing the log, ZIL replay can
apply the first part of a write without the rest, leaving the write
torn at an arbitrary 8-byte-aligned offset.  This exposes a state that
no reader could have observed before the crash: new data up to the
tear, old data after it.  While POSIX doesn't specify anything before
fsync() completes, ZFS intends to preserve the atomicity of writes as
much as possible: writes within a single block are intended to be
atomic, with larger writes torn only on recordsize boundaries.

The problem occurs with WR_NEED_COPY TX_WRITE records.  When such a
record does not fit in the current log block, zil_lwb_assign() splits
it into several TX_WRITE records in consecutive log blocks.  If the
system crashes after the first of those blocks is on disk but before
all of them are, zil_parse() stops at the missing block, but replay
has already applied the pieces before it.  Files are affected for
async writes that a later fsync() commits, and for writes of any size
with a slog.  Zvols are affected too, where a tear inside a sector
breaks the sector atomicity that consumers of a block device expect.

Mark the pieces of a split write in the log, and have replay apply the
write only once it holds every piece:

- Rename the unused lr_blkoff field of lr_write_t to lr_flags.  Set
  LR_WRITE_MORE (bit 63) on every piece but the last, and
  LR_WRITE_CONT (bit 62) on every piece but the first.

- If zl_get_data() returns ENOENT for a piece (the file was truncated
  below it or removed since the write), log that piece as zeros.
  Otherwise replay would drop the whole write, losing the other
  pieces' data.  The TX_TRUNCATE or TX_REMOVE that follows undoes the
  zeros.  So that this happens only for a file that is gone,
  zfs_get_data() now reports any other zfs_zget() failure as EIO
  rather than ENOENT (such as EBUSY on FreeBSD during unmount, or
  ENOENT when no znode could be allocated for an object that still
  exists), and zil_lwb_commit() waits for the txg to sync instead.

- Replay gathers the pieces in its record buffer.  A piece joins the
  pending write only if its lrc_seq is the next one and its txg,
  object and offset match; otherwise the pending write and the piece
  are dropped.  The last piece replays the gathered write as one
  TX_WRITE.  Any other record, or the end of the log, drops a pending
  write.  On an opposite-endian host, whose log blocks the ARC swaps
  as 8-byte words, each piece's data is swapped back as it's
  gathered.  If replay by older software was interrupted partway through
  a split write, the first record left to replay is a later piece;
  replay treats it as the start of a write.

- Replay gathers up to 32M of data (DMU_MAX_ACCESS / 2), as much as
  zfs_write() and zvol_write() apply in one transaction, and so log in
  one record.  The replay buffer grows by a record header to hold it.
  A larger write can't be applied in one transaction anyway; replay
  applies it in parts.  If a piece doesn't fit, replay applies the
  gathered data up to its last multiple of SPA_MAXBLOCKSIZE (always a
  block boundary) first.  If that replay is interrupted, it resumes
  with the piece after the last one wholly applied.

No feature flag is needed.  lr_blkoff was last set by 2007-2009
software, which read it at replay only for WR_INDIRECT records, and
later software ignores it.  So old logs replay as before, and older
software replays new logs as before (still torn).

New kstats zil_replay_split_merged (writes) and
zil_replay_split_dropped (pieces), global and per dataset, count what
replay did.  zdb -ivvv prints the markers.

This is a port of the illumos fix, with these OpenZFS differences:

- OpenZFS already assigns lrc_seq before calling zl_get_data(), so a
  piece it leaves out leaves a gap in the sequence.

- illumos splits a record after fetching the data for its first piece,
  so if that fails the whole record is skipped, and only later pieces
  are logged as zeros.  OpenZFS splits it in zil_lwb_assign(), and the
  pieces' data may be fetched concurrently by different threads, so
  the first piece is logged as zeros too.  The file ends up the same.

- illumos gathers up to DMU_MAX_ACCESS, which is 32M there.

- The zvol tests set sync=always, since FreeBSD's GEOM zvols ignore
  O_SYNC, and write with O_DIRECT so that Linux submits the write as
  one request.  Like slog_replay_volume, they retry a busy export.

New ZTS tests in functional/slog lose a log block by overwriting it
with zeros, and check the data and the replay kstats:
slog_replay_fs_split_write, slog_replay_volume_split_write,
slog_replay_volume_split_boundary and slog_replay_fs_split_truncate.

