Backport notes (zfs-2.4.4): upstream applied this before c0688a275,
which this tree already carries (fca938dff).  Every conflict comes from
that order or from master-only test changes, and is resolved so the
result is master's 7c3d85aaf followed by c0688a275:
- zil.c, zil_replay(): keep c0688a275's err next to split_lr, and after
  the replay parse drop a pending split write, free ZIL_REPLAY_BUFSZ,
  then clear zl_replay_write2.  zil_replay() is now the same as master's
  at c0688a275.  The split functions go between zil_replay_error() and
  c0688a275's zil_replay_scan_blk(), as on master.
- common.run, Makefile.am: the four new tests join 2.4's slog lists,
  which already hold c0688a275's tests.  Master's Makefile.am context
  also has the snapdir group (73ba07c67, "ZTS: create snapdir test
  group"), which 2.4 lacks.  Both slog lists now match master's at
  c0688a275.
Every other hunk applies as is.  The code also applies to plain
zfs-2.4.4 without conflict.  The functions it changes, and those it
relies on (zil_parse(), zil_read_log_data(), zil_itx_create(),
zil_lwb_write_issue(), zfs_get_done(), zvol_get_data(),
zfs_replay_write(), zvol_replay_write(), the dataset kstats, zdb's
print_log_block()), are the same in 2.4.4 as on master before it.
zfs_write() and zil_process_commit_list() differ only by master-only
changes it does not use (z_seq, uncached I/O, ZILSAXATTR activation).
DMU_MAX_ACCESS is 64M in 2.4 too, and zfs_write() and zvol_write()
write at most DMU_MAX_ACCESS / 2 per transaction, so
ZIL_REPLAY_WRITE_MAX still covers one logged write.  The ZTS helpers
the new tests use (kstat, log_must_busy, block_device_wait, slog
setup/cleanup) are the same on Linux as on master.

